{"page_content": "Flame Design PI Rich Axelbaum explained: \n\u201cOne of the fundamental goals of Flame Design is to achieve sooting limits.  We are unsure \nif we will be able to attain a range of sooting flames with the current ambient fuel \nconstraints.  One way to increase soot formation, and thus the range of the test matrix, \nis via higher pressures, so this capability can help to ensure we meet our science goals.  \nAnother fundamental goal of Flame Design is to understand flame extinction.  Our recent \nanalysis suggests that pressure is a critical variable when predicting flame extinction, and \nin can influence flame chemistry in ways that we had not anticipated.  Obtaining \nextinction data over a wide range of pressures could also be valuable for spacecraft fire \nsafety.\u201d  \n \nAn additional normal-flames test day was added to the start of the second round of testing (even \nthough that was focused on inverse flames) to acquire data useful for calibrating the Thin Filament \nPyrometry (TFP) measurements.  With that goal, the added test day, conducted on 28 Oct. 2021", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 19}}